sections.body:
Here are two numbers from the print shop's app: the weight of a parcel in grams, and the number of sheets in it. Both are `Long`:

```java
static String ship(long grams) { return "shipped " + grams + " g"; }

long grams = 2_500;
long sheets = 500;
ship(sheets);                  // compiles: both are long
```

```kotlin
fun ship(grams: Long): String = "shipped $grams g"

val grams: Long = 2_500
val sheets: Long = 500
ship(sheets)                   // compiles: both are Long
```

The compiler can't tell them apart, so `ship(sheets)` happily posts a parcel that weighs 500 sheets. A wrapper class, `class Grams(val value: Long)`, would catch the swap. It works, and every weight is now an extra object on the heap, the memory where the JVM keeps objects.

Kotlin's **value class** keeps the wrapper's type for the compiler and, wherever it can, drops the object. At run time only the wrapped value is left, the underlying type (`Long` here). In the print shop, the unit on the job sheet is the value class. The clerk, who is the compiler, checks it at the desk. The machines in the back room, which are the JVM at run time, only see the number.

Here is the wrapper in both languages:

```java
// Java's closest shape today: a record. It's a real object, created on every new.
record Grams(long value) {
    Grams {
        if (value < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("negative weight: " + value);
    }
}
```

```kotlin
@JvmInline
value class Grams(val value: Long) {
    init {
        require(value >= 0) { "negative weight: $value" }
    }

    operator fun plus(other: Grams): Grams = Grams(value + other.value)

    override fun toString(): String = "$value g"
}

fun ship(weight: Grams): String = "shipped $weight"

ship(sheets)   // error: argument type mismatch: actual type is 'Long', but 'Grams' was expected.
```

The rules are short. On the JVM you write `@JvmInline value class` with exactly one property in the primary constructor. You may add `init` blocks, functions, secondary constructors, and properties without backing fields, that is, without a stored value of their own (so no `lateinit` and no delegation). A value class can implement interfaces (types that only name methods and properties), can't extend a class, and is always final.

At run time most of it disappears. `javap -p`, the JDK tool that lists what a class file contains, shows what the compiler built for the demo:

```text
public static final java.lang.String ship-P0HeBCA(long);   // takes a plain long
public static long constructor-impl(long);                // Grams(...) runs init here
public static final long plus-vak4SgM(long, long);        // a member, now static on two longs
public static final Grams box-impl(long);                 // used only when a box is needed
```

`Grams(150)` compiles to a call to `constructor-impl`, which runs your `init` block and hands back the same `long`. No object is created. `plus` turned into a static method that adds two longs. The `Grams` class still exists, with one `long` field, for the moments when a real object is needed. The odd hash after `ship` is the compiler's work too, and a later section explains it.

Compare a type alias. `typealias GramsAlias = Long` gives you a second name and nothing else. The docs say aliases are assignment-compatible with their underlying type, and value classes are not. In the demo, `sheets: Long` goes straight into `shipAlias` and compiles. Use aliases to shorten long type names. Use value classes where mixing up two values would be a bug.

You already use value classes from the standard library. `UInt`, `ULong`, `kotlin.time.Duration` and `kotlin.Result` are all declared this way, and `javap` shows the same `box-impl` and `unbox-impl` on each.

**When two values share a type but must never be swapped, give each one its own value class.**

"Wherever it can" is doing a lot of work in that description, though. Let's find the places where it can't.

code.src:
package workbench.kotlin.l02.demo

/** A value class: a Grams in your code, a long in the bytecode. */
@JvmInline
value class Grams(val value: Long) {
    init {
        require(value >= 0) { "negative weight: $value" }
    }

    operator fun plus(other: Grams): Grams = Grams(value + other.value)

    override fun toString(): String = "$value g"
}

/** A type alias is only a second name. Any Long is accepted where a GramsAlias is expected. */
typealias GramsAlias = Long

fun ship(weight: Grams): String = "shipped $weight"

@JvmName("shipFromJava")
fun shipJava(weight: Grams): String = "shipped $weight"

fun shipAlias(weight: GramsAlias): String = "shipped $weight g (alias)"

fun main() {
    val parcel = Grams(150) + Grams(49)
    println(ship(parcel))
    val sheets: Long = 500
    println(shipAlias(sheets))         // compiles: a sheet count is a Long, so it is a GramsAlias too
    try {
        Grams(-1)
    } catch (e: IllegalArgumentException) {
        println("rejected: ${e.message}")
    }

    // Wrong move. Uncomment, predict the error, then run.
    // println(ship(sheets))
}

